{#
  landing.html.j2 — grouped landing page for the multi-user web-terminal stack.

  Rendered by render_web_terminals() and served as a plain static file by
  nginx — there is no app route behind this page, so it must stay self-contained
  (inline CSS, no external assets, no build step).

  Template variables (contract with the caller):

    facility_name: str, optional
      Shown in the page header. Falls back to "OSPREY" when empty/undefined.

    theme_blocks: list[dict], required
      The design-system token values to bake into the inline stylesheet, built
      by render.py's `_landing_theme_blocks()` from the deployment's
      `web.theme`:
        {
          "media": str | None,      # e.g. "(prefers-color-scheme: light)"
          "color_scheme": str,      # "dark" | "light"
          "variables": [(name, value), ...],   # "--bg-primary", "#091720"
        }
      A family-valued `web.theme` yields two blocks (dark under `:root`, light
      behind the media query); a concrete-id one yields a single unconditional
      block, honoring the pinned mode. This page has no theme picker and no
      localStorage — it is shown before anyone has identified themselves, so it
      always uses the deployment-level theme, never a per-user one.
      NOT `|e`-escaped: these are CSS, not HTML, and HTML escaping would corrupt
      values while doing nothing about the only real hazard (a `</style>`
      sequence). render.py pattern-checks every value instead, and the values
      originate in the framework's own token tree rather than operator config.

    groups: list[dict], required
      Ordered list of groups to render:
        {
          "label": str,            # group heading, e.g. "Terminals" or "Dashboards"
          "items": list[dict],     # [{"label": str, "url": str, "sublabel"?: str}, ...]
          "variant": str,          # OPTIONAL, only "tray" is recognized
        }
      ONE shared loop renders every group in this list — the `users` group is not
      special-cased beyond how its data arrives:
        - The `users` group is auto-populated by the caller from the roster at
          `modules.web_terminals.users[]`: one item per user, `{label: <username>,
          url: <that user's terminal origin>}`, plus an OPTIONAL `sublabel` holding
          that user's persona name when one is in effect (a bare-string roster with
          no persona omits the key entirely).
        - All other groups are operator-supplied `{label, url}` lists straight out of
          `modules.web_terminals.landing`.
      `sublabel` is optional on every item: when present it renders as a secondary
      persona badge on the card; when absent the card renders exactly as a plain
      `{label, url}` card (bracket-subscripting a missing key yields Jinja
      `Undefined`, which is falsy — see the `{% if item["sublabel"] %}` guard).
      A group whose `items` list is empty renders nothing — no heading, no empty
      section. This is the ONLY suppression rule, and it is generic to every group:
      it's what makes the 0-user case disappear cleanly (an empty/omitted `users`
      group) while any other configured group still renders normally.

      `variant` is optional on every group and drives presentation only. The one
      recognized value, "tray", renders that section inside an accent-edged panel
      instead of flat on the page: it is how the caller marks a group as a
      different KIND of destination — a standalone service the deployment runs
      (a logbook terminal, a dashboard app) rather than a person's login. The
      page then reads as people on top, services below. Any other value, and the
      absent case, render the plain section, so a caller that never sets it
      produces exactly the markup this template produced before the key existed.
      Compared against the literal "tray" rather than tested for truthiness, so
      an unrecognized variant degrades to the plain rendering instead of picking
      up tray styling by accident.

  Escaping: this template is rendered with a Jinja2 Environment that has autoescape
  ON (HTML). `label`/`url` are still explicitly piped through `|e` wherever they're
  interpolated, as defense-in-depth per the framework's escapeHtml convention —
  `url` in particular is treated as untrusted operator input, not just `label`.

  URL scheme: HTML-escaping alone does not neutralize a `url` like
  `"javascript:...\"` — it would still render as a live, clickable link that
  executes script on click. `url` is checked against an http(s)/site-relative
  allowlist before it is ever used in an `href` (same check gates the display
  text); a `url` with any other scheme renders its card inert (no `<a>`, no
  navigation) instead of being dropped, so a misconfigured group is still visible.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>{{ (facility_name|default("", true))|e or "OSPREY" }} Web Terminals</title>
  <style>
    {#- Design-system token values, baked at render time from the deployment's
       `web.theme`. Two blocks when web.theme named a family (dark under :root,
       light behind a prefers-color-scheme query, so the page follows the
       viewer's OS just as the terminals do); one block and no query when it
       named a concrete id, which pins the mode. Values come from the framework's
       own token tree and are pattern-checked in _landing_theme_blocks() before
       reaching this <style>. #}
    {%- for block in theme_blocks|default([]) %}
    {%- set pad = "  " if block["media"] else "" %}
    {%- if block["media"] %}
    @media {{ block["media"] }} {
    {%- endif %}
    {{ pad }}:root {
    {{ pad }}  color-scheme: {{ block["color_scheme"] }};
    {%- for name, value in block["variables"] %}
    {{ pad }}  {{ name }}: {{ value }};
    {%- endfor %}
    {{ pad }}}
    {%- if block["media"] %}
    }
    {%- endif %}
    {%- endfor %}
    * { box-sizing: border-box; }
    body {
      margin: 0;
      min-height: 100vh;
      background: var(--bg-primary);
      color: var(--text-primary);
      font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
    }
    .landing-header {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 960px;
      padding: 2.5rem 1.5rem 1rem;
    }
    .landing-title {
      margin: 0;
      font-size: 1rem;
      font-weight: 400;
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 0.75rem;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }
    .landing-logo {
      font-weight: 700;
      font-size: 1.35rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      color: var(--color-accent);
    }
    .landing-subtitle {
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
    }
    .landing-main {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 960px;
      flex: 1;
      padding: 0.5rem 1.5rem 3rem;
    }
    .landing-empty {
      margin-top: 2rem;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      text-align: center;
    }
    .landing-group {
      margin-top: 2rem;
    }
    .landing-group-label {
      margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
    }
    /* A `variant: "tray"` section: the same heading and card grid, enclosed in
       an accent-edged panel so a standalone service reads as a different kind
       of destination from a person's terminal.

       Deliberately NO background fill. Every candidate surface token collides
       with something in at least one shipped theme — --bg-elevated is the card
       HOVER color, and --bg-secondary equals elevated in `light` (hovering a
       card would dissolve it into the tray), equals --bg-panel in
       `high-contrast-dark` (cards invisible against it) and sits ABOVE panel in
       `retro-dark` (cards sunken into it, inverting every other card on the
       page). Leaving the page's own --bg-primary showing through introduces no
       new color at all, so the cards inside keep exactly the surface
       relationship — and exactly the hover — that the cards above the tray
       have, in all eight themes. The accent border carries the "this is
       something else" signal instead, and it is theme-safe by construction:
       every theme defines --border-accent as a composite of its own accent. */
    .landing-tray {
      margin-top: 2.25rem;
      padding: 1.25rem 1.25rem 1.4rem;
      border: 1px solid var(--border-accent);
      border-radius: 14px;
    }
    /* The heading is already inside the tray's own padding, so it does not need
       the extra top margin .landing-group carries to separate flat sections. */
    .landing-tray > .landing-group-label {
      margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
    }
    /* Accent mark. An empty `content` with a background, NOT a "◆" glyph:
       generated content IS folded into the accessible name, so a character
       here would have every card in the tray announce a stray "black diamond"
       ahead of its actual label. A box has no text to announce, and it also
       renders identically everywhere rather than depending on which font on
       the viewer's machine happens to carry the codepoint. */
    .landing-tray .landing-card-label::before {
      content: "";
      display: inline-block;
      width: 0.4rem;
      height: 0.4rem;
      margin-right: 0.55rem;
      vertical-align: 0.04rem;
      background: var(--color-accent);
      transform: rotate(45deg);
    }
    .landing-cards {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));
      gap: 0.75rem;
    }
    .landing-card {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.3rem;
      padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
      background: var(--bg-panel);
      border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
      border-radius: 10px;
      box-shadow: var(--shadow-dropdown);
      text-decoration: none;
      color: inherit;
      transition: border-color 120ms ease, transform 120ms ease, background 120ms ease;
      min-width: 0;
    }
    .landing-card:hover,
    .landing-card:focus-visible {
      border-color: var(--border-accent);
      background: var(--bg-elevated);
      transform: translateY(-1px);
    }
    .landing-card:focus-visible {
      outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
      outline-offset: 2px;
    }
    .landing-card-inert {
      cursor: not-allowed;
      opacity: 0.6;
      border-style: dashed;
    }
    .landing-card-inert:hover {
      border-color: var(--border-default);
      background: var(--bg-panel);
      transform: none;
    }
    .landing-card-label {
      font-size: 1rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      color: var(--text-primary);
      overflow: hidden;
      text-overflow: ellipsis;
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
    .landing-card-sublabel {
      align-self: flex-start;
      max-width: 100%;
      margin: 0.05rem 0 0.1rem;
      padding: 0.12rem 0.55rem;
      border: 1px solid var(--border-accent);
      border-radius: 999px;
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.05em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--color-accent);
      overflow: hidden;
      text-overflow: ellipsis;
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
    .landing-card-url {
      font-family: ui-monospace, "JetBrains Mono", monospace;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      overflow: hidden;
      text-overflow: ellipsis;
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
    /* Facility notices, at the bottom of the page everyone passes through on
       the way to a terminal. Collapsed, because a person who has read them is
       opening a terminal rather than reading them again, and an expanded wall
       of text above the fold would train exactly the skimming it is trying to
       prevent. Native <details>: no script on this page. */
    .landing-notices {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 960px;
      margin-top: 2.5rem;
      padding: 0 1.5rem;
    }
    .landing-notice > summary {
      cursor: pointer;
      padding: 0.7rem 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default);
      font-size: 0.82rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
    }
    .landing-notice > summary:hover {
      color: var(--text-primary);
    }
    .landing-notice-body {
      padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
      font-size: 0.86rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      max-width: 46rem;
    }
    .landing-notice-body h2,
    .landing-notice-body h3 {
      margin: 1.4rem 0 0.4rem;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--color-accent);
    }
    .landing-notice-body ul {
      margin: 0.3rem 0;
      padding-left: 1.1rem;
    }
    .landing-notice-body li {
      margin: 0.25rem 0;
    }
    .landing-notice-body strong {
      color: var(--text-primary);
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    .landing-footer {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 960px;
      padding: 1rem 1.5rem 2rem;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      text-align: center;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <header class="landing-header">
    <h1 class="landing-title">
      <span class="landing-logo">OSPREY</span>
      <span class="landing-subtitle">{{ (facility_name|default("", true))|e or "Web Terminals" }}</span>
    </h1>
  </header>

  <main class="landing-main">
    {#- Generic emptiness check: true only when every group's items list is empty. #}
    {% set ns = namespace(any_visible=false) %}
    {% for group in groups|default([]) %}
      {% if group["items"] %}{% set ns.any_visible = true %}{% endif %}
    {% endfor %}

    {% if not ns.any_visible %}
      <p class="landing-empty">No terminals or links are configured yet.</p>
    {% endif %}

    {% for group in groups|default([]) %}
      {% if group["items"] %}
      {#- Equality against the one recognized value, not truthiness: an
         unrecognized variant must fall back to the plain section rather than
         emit a class no stylesheet in this page defines. The class name is a
         literal chosen here, so nothing operator-supplied reaches the class
         attribute. #}
      <section class="landing-group{{ " landing-tray" if group["variant"] == "tray" else "" }}">
        <h2 class="landing-group-label">{{ group["label"]|e }}</h2>
        <div class="landing-cards">
          {% for item in group["items"] %}
          {#- |e alone escapes markup but not the URL *scheme* — a raw "javascript:"/
             "data:"/"vbscript:" url would still render as a live, clickable
             script-execution link. Allowlist by inclusion of known-safe schemes
             (http, https, site-relative) rather than by excluding bad ones, and
             reuse the SAME normalized value for both the href and the display text.
             A leading "//" is EXCLUDED from the site-relative branch: browsers
             resolve "//host/path" as a scheme-relative off-origin URL (same as
             "https://host/path"), so "//evil.com/x" is a bypass, not a relative
             path, and must be treated as unsafe like javascript:/data:. #}
          {% set raw_url = item["url"] if item["url"] is string else "" %}
          {% set is_relative = raw_url.startswith("/") and not raw_url.startswith("//") %}
          {% set is_http = raw_url.startswith("http://") or raw_url.startswith("https://") %}
          {% set safe_url = raw_url if (is_http or is_relative) else "" %}
          {% if safe_url %}
          <a class="landing-card" href="{{ safe_url|e }}">
            <span class="landing-card-label">{{ item["label"]|e }}</span>
            {%- if item["sublabel"] %}
            <span class="landing-card-sublabel">{{ item["sublabel"]|e }}</span>
            {%- endif %}
            <span class="landing-card-url">{{ safe_url|e }}</span>
          </a>
          {% else %}
          <div class="landing-card landing-card-inert" title="Unsupported link scheme">
            <span class="landing-card-label">{{ item["label"]|e }}</span>
            {%- if item["sublabel"] %}
            <span class="landing-card-sublabel">{{ item["sublabel"]|e }}</span>
            {%- endif %}
            <span class="landing-card-url">unsupported link</span>
          </div>
          {% endif %}
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      </section>
      {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
  </main>

  {#- Facility notices. Each entry in `modules.web_terminals.landing.notices`
     is one markdown file; its `# H1` becomes the summary label and the rest
     becomes the panel, so a facility adds a section by adding a file. The
     body is already HTML by the time it reaches here (rendered in
     render.py::_render_notice) and is the ONE unescaped value on this page —
     everything else, including the footer, still goes through `|e`. #}
  {% if notices %}
  <section class="landing-notices">
    {% for notice in notices %}
    <details class="landing-notice" id="{{ notice['id']|e }}">
      <summary>{{ notice['title']|e }}</summary>
      <div class="landing-notice-body">{{ notice['body_html']|safe }}</div>
    </details>
    {% endfor %}
  </section>
  {% endif %}

  {% if footer %}
  <footer class="landing-footer">
    {{ footer|e }}
  </footer>
  {% endif %}

</body>
</html>
